l(\"$B$\",B,N); label(\"$C$\",C,E); label(\"$D$\",D,E);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"127b1ffef7a3bdec41382bbd4863df29f19b9f3d483fb27276d96a063b34fcc0"},"seconds":19.261779069900513,"seed":589420671130291307,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, let's analyze the given information and the diagram. We have an isosceles triangle \\(ABC\\) with angles \\( \\angle BAC = 35^\\circ \\) and \\( \\angle BCA = 35^\\circ \\). This means that \\( \\angle ABC \\) is the remaining angle in the triangle. Since the sum of the angles in a triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\), we can calculate \\( \\angle ABC \\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC - \\angle BCA = 180^\\circ - 35^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 110^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the measure of \\( \\angle CDA \\). Notice that \\(D\\) is a point on the circle, and \\(A, B, C, D\\) are points on the circumference of the circle. This means that \\(ABCD\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral. In a cyclic quadrilateral, the opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, \\( \\angle CDA \\) is the supplement of \\( \\angle ABC \\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle CDA = 180^\\circ - \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - 110^\\circ = 70^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the measure of \\( \\angle CDA \\) is \\(\\boxed{70}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{70}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
